# Performance and SEO

## Core Web Vitals Targets

- LCP <= 2.5s
- INP <= 200ms
- CLS <= 0.1

## Performance Rules

- Optimize the LCP element (usually hero media/headline). Use correct image priority, sizing, and modern formats when available.
- Reserve space for media/embeds/async content to prevent CLS (`width`/`height` or `aspect-ratio`).
- Break up long main-thread tasks; debounce expensive handlers; defer non-critical scripts.
- Prefer framework image/font/script optimizations.
- Self-host and subset fonts when possible; use non-blocking font loading strategies approved by the project.
- Code-split by route/feature; avoid shipping unused interactive weight to content pages.
- Do not add heavy dependencies for trivial utilities.
- Animate transform/opacity when possible; avoid layout-thrashing animations.
- Lazy-load below-the-fold or heavy widgets only when it improves UX and does not harm critical paths.
- Treat third-party scripts (analytics, chat, tags) as performance and security risk; load them intentionally.

## SEO and Content Structure

- Maintain logical heading hierarchy and meaningful document structure.
- Keep titles, descriptions, canonicals, Open Graph/Twitter metadata, and robots directives correct for the framework.
- Prefer indexable server-rendered or statically available critical content when SEO matters.
- Use descriptive link text; avoid “click here”.
- Add structured data only when product-relevant and valid.
- Keep social preview images and share metadata correct after UI changes.

## Internationalization and Content

- Preserve existing i18n frameworks and message catalogs.
- No hardcoded user-facing strings when translations are used.
- Layouts must tolerate longer translations and compact scripts.
- Support RTL when required; prefer logical CSS properties over physical left/right assumptions when available.
- Use locale-aware date/number/currency formatting (`Intl` or project utilities).
- Avoid concatenated translated sentences that break grammar in other languages.

## Resilience and Observability

- Fail visibly and safely. Prefer useful empty/error states over blank screens or infinite spinners.
- Error boundaries should recover gracefully without leaking sensitive internals.
- Distinguish network, auth, permission, not-found, and validation failures when relevant.
- Retries should be bounded and non-destructive.
- Keep logging structured and free of secrets.
- Preserve existing monitoring/analytics instrumentation unless asked to change it.
